BACKGROUND = [
    {"id": "bg-correspondence", "after": "section-1-p1", "title": "The black hole/string correspondence", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>A black hole of mass $M$ in $D$ dimensions has a horizon that shrinks as it radiates, and its temperature rises. When the horizon reaches the string length $l_s$, curvature is of string size and general relativity stops being reliable. Susskind, and Horowitz and Polchinski, noticed that at this point the black hole's entropy is comparable to that of a single highly excited string of the same mass, which grows linearly with $M$. This correspondence principle suggests that the black hole does not end abruptly but turns into strings, perhaps a ball of string bound by its own gravity. In the Euclidean language used here, the black hole and that self-gravitating string are two saddles of the same thermal partition function, and the claim is that one deforms into the other as the temperature changes. The paper asks whether the same picture survives when the black hole rotates.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-imaginary", "after": "subsec-grand_can-p2", "title": "Imaginary angular velocity as a twisted circle", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>The grand canonical trace $\operatorname{Tr}\,e^{-\beta(H-\Omega J)}$ becomes, as a Euclidean path integral, an integral over fields on space times a circle of length $\beta$. The factor $e^{\beta\Omega J}$ is a rotation; for imaginary $\Omega$, with $\beta\Omega=2\pi i\nu$, it rotates the plane of $J$ by the real angle $2\pi\nu$. The trace then glues the end of Euclidean time to its start after this rotation: fields obey $\Phi(\tau+\beta)=e^{-2\pi i\nu}\Phi(\tau)$ for the complex coordinate of that plane. Space stays locally flat; only the identification is twisted. Since $e^{2\pi iJ}$ is $1$ for integer spins and $(-1)^F$ for half-integer ones, $\nu$ is periodic with period 1 for bosonic theories and 2 for theories with fermions, where $\nu=1$ inserts $(-1)^F$. A rotating Euclidean black hole is a real metric for such imaginary angular velocity. The paper's thermal strings, black holes and string stars all live with this twisted boundary condition.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-hagedorn", "after": "subsec-micro-p2", "title": "Why string states grow exponentially", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>A free closed string's mass is set by its oscillator level $N$, with $\alpha'M^2\approx4N$ per side. The number of states at level $N$ is the number of ways to share $N$ among the oscillators of all transverse directions, a partition problem. Its asymptotics follow from the Cardy formula: the count grows like $e^{2\pi\sqrt{cN/6}}$, with $c$ the number of transverse worldsheet fields. Since $\sqrt N$ is proportional to $M$, the number of string states grows exponentially in the mass, $e^{\beta_HM}$, and the entropy linearly. A gas of strings at temperature $T$ weighs each state by $e^{-M/T}$; above $T_H=1/\beta_H$ the growth wins and the partition function diverges. This is the Hagedorn temperature, $1/(4\pi\sqrt{\alpha'})$ for bosonic strings. The paper finds the same exponential growth when the states are also weighted by the phase of their angular momentum, with a rate that depends on that phase.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-twisted", "after": "subsec-euc_hag-p4", "title": "Twisted sectors and shifted zero-point energy", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>When a theory is quotiented by a symmetry, an orbifold, closed strings need only close up to that symmetry: these extra states form twisted sectors. Here a string wound once around the thermal circle must return with its rotation-plane coordinate rotated by $e^{2\pi i\nu}$, so its oscillator frequencies shift from integers $n$ to $|n+\nu|$. The mass of a string state includes the zero-point energy of all its oscillators, a divergent sum regulated by the Riemann zeta function: $\sum_{n\ge1}n=-\frac1{12}$, and the shifted sum $\sum_n(n+\nu)$ gives a $\nu$-dependent answer. A single twisted complex boson therefore changes the ground-state energy of the wound string by a quadratic polynomial in $\nu$. Since the lightest wound string's mass decides the Hagedorn temperature, this zero-point shift is what makes $R_H$ depend on $\nu$ in the paper's worldsheet derivation.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-gso", "after": "subsec-euc_hag-p7", "title": "The GSO projection and the lightest level", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>The Ramond–Neveu–Schwarz superstring has worldsheet fermions, and its spectrum contains a tachyon at the lowest level. The Gliozzi–Scherk–Olive (GSO) projection keeps only states of a definite worldsheet fermion number; it removes that tachyon and makes the theory supersymmetric and consistent. On a thermal circle the target-space fermions are antiperiodic, and wound strings then obey a different projection: the lowest level $N=0$ survives, and it is this state that turns tachyonic at the Hagedorn temperature. With periodic fermions, the supersymmetric case, the usual projection applies and the lowest allowed level is $N=1/2$. Inserting the rotation $e^{2\pi i\nu J}$ interpolates between the two, since $\nu=1$ makes the fermions periodic. The paper therefore has to follow which level is allowed as $\nu$ varies, and finds it changes at $|\nu|=1/2$.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-melvin", "after": "subsec-Atick_W-p2", "title": "Melvin space as a Kaluza–Klein flux tube", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>In Kaluza–Klein reduction, a metric on space times a circle is written as $G_{tt}(dt-A_idx^i)^2$ plus a spatial metric: the off-diagonal components become a gauge field $A$, the circle's size a scalar, and momentum around the circle is the charge. Melvin space is flat space in which going around the circle rotates a plane by an angle $2\pi\nu$. In coordinates where the identification is untwisted, the metric acquires a cross term between the circle and the angle of the plane, so $A_\phi$ is non-zero: a magnetic field, concentrated near the rotation axis and falling off far from it, together with a varying circle size $G_{tt}=R^2+\nu^2\rho^2$. This is a magnetic flux tube, or fluxbrane. A string wound on the circle feels it through $G_{tt}$, which grows away from the axis and confines it near $\rho=0$, the mass term the paper finds.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-hp", "after": "sec-sss-p1", "title": "The Horowitz–Polchinski string star", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>Just below the Hagedorn temperature the thermal winding mode $\chi$ is light, with $m^2\propto R-R_H$, and can be described by a field theory in $d$ dimensions together with gravity, as Horowitz and Polchinski did. Its mass depends on the size of the thermal circle, which the Newtonian potential $\varphi$ changes: where the potential is negative the circle shrinks and the winding mode gets lighter. A condensate of $\chi$ sources $\varphi$, and $\varphi$ in turn lowers the mass of $\chi$, so the condensate can bind itself. The equations, $\nabla^2\chi=m^2\chi+\kappa\varphi\chi$ and $\nabla^2\varphi\propto|\chi|^2$, are scale invariant: the solution has size $1/m$ and amplitude of order $m^2$. Normalizable solutions exist for $3\le d\le5$ spatial dimensions; for $d\ge6$ they collapse. Their entropy obeys $S\approx\beta_HM$, like free strings. This Horowitz–Polchinski solution is the original string star, and the paper perturbs it in the twist $\nu$.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-myers-perry", "after": "subsec-black-hole-string-star-corr-p1", "title": "Myers–Perry black holes", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>In four dimensions the rotating vacuum black hole is Kerr's. Myers and Perry found its generalisation to $D$ spacetime dimensions, where a black hole can rotate independently in each of $\lfloor(D-1)/2\rfloor$ planes. With rotation in a single plane, the solution has a mass parameter $\mu$ and a rotation parameter $a$, and its horizon radius $r_h$ solves $r_h^2+a^2=\mu/r_h^{D-5}$. Its mass, entropy and angular momentum follow, with $J\propto Ma$, as do its temperature and horizon angular velocity $\Omega=a/(r_h^2+a^2)$. For small $\Omega$ everything can be expanded about Schwarzschild, and the horizon flattens, with eccentricity proportional to $\Omega$. In $D\ge6$ such black holes can spin without bound and become very flat, the ultraspinning regime. The paper uses only the slow, single-plane case, extrapolated to string-scale temperature, to compare with its rotating string star.</p>"""},
]
